104
00:06:37,088 --> 00:06:50,960
A diffusion model is a model that takes some random noise and through iterative computation can go from noise to something that's really high-dimensional, that's something really highly like defined and have very fine grained feature.

105
00:06:51,310 --> 00:06:55,968
If you think about generating images, right, you want sharp edges, sharp corners, color contrast.

106
00:06:56,334 --> 00:07:03,648
And for robotics, even though you're not generating image, you're generating actions that needs to be very precise, very like smooth, right?

107
00:07:03,728 --> 00:07:09,328
So it has kind of the same kind of uh consideration decided as generating image.

108
00:07:09,408 --> 00:07:18,948
So that's why, from first principle, you know, the whole field, not just us, have been using this kind of diffusion transformer architecture to train robotics foundation models.

109
00:07:19,374 --> 00:07:24,836
You need the transformer aspect because transformer is the thing that can just ingest the most amount of data, can learn.

110
00:07:25,134 --> 00:07:32,964
Then diffusion helps you to refine the outcome to get to the smooth, precise, fine-grained motion you need to actually execute on the robot.

111
00:07:33,168 --> 00:07:37,888
So that's the architecture that goes behind a lot of our recent breakthroughs.

112
00:07:38,158 --> 00:07:43,108
And we and we've covered flow matching before on the podcast, is mostly an efficiency speed up.

113
00:07:43,248 --> 00:07:46,820
Like let's say you normally would take 40 to 100 diffusion steps.

